{
  "rules": [
    "Strong positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–10+ €/t). Apply a downward level shift to the entire path, larger when recent history is elevated (80+).",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: after a peak in history, the model continues projecting sideways/up while the market sells off. Add a regime check: if last 5–10 days show a rollover (lower highs / negative slope), force an immediate downtrend rather than flat/mean-reverting up.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: when history includes a sharp run-up, the model extrapolates strength and misses subsequent drawdowns. Damp positive momentum signals (cap upside continuation) and increase probability/size of pullbacks following large recent gains.",
    "Insufficient downside volatility: realized path drops faster and deeper than forecast (model too smooth). Widen downside moves—apply asymmetric error correction that increases negative daily changes during bearish regimes.",
    "Horizon effect: bias compounds with horizon (errors grow with lead time). Use horizon-dependent correction: small downward adjustment at 1–7d, larger at 8–20d, largest at 21–30d, plus steeper negative drift when regime is bearish."
  ]
}
